b/ref 1079 #uuid "ec791608-10e6-5073-b263-73fdaf240d93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543467 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543467 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543467 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186543242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543467 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa17f357-450d-5ebf-a036-6faafdfbae46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0135fc07-6118-52f5-9b57-2dcf9eca07ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543470 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}$ has no solution.\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543484 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1dd38a3d7e9969b9cc74c043261fd2f4d3ee1c1293a623fd812e31256b4ec766.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the curve $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}.$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C$ and $D$, as shown. Find the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22a4714b-c9b9-595d-bd56-cdf122336677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543484 #edb/ref 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={210}^{∘},\\,{330}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={210}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={270}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D$ is \n\n$$\n210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49fd94b7-d87e-5482-b74a-14a44acf4a47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543499 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543500 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a638344-d920-5adc-900d-8c273723b689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543500 #edb/ref 1074 "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solution to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  is $x={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x={30}^{∘},{150}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=30\\cdot 150=4\\,500.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543506 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543506 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543506 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543506 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543506 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c63aa2a-bac5-5697-a40d-14aec41163e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543506 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={120}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nTaking into account that ${90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}$ are excluded values, and combining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 300=36\\,000.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68f4987d-ed26-5738-89d9-828f518f37d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543513 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ or $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=0,\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pm \\pi .$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81da0bad-95e8-53a0-9f19-ddc55b628c5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543522 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={120}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nTaking into account that ${90}^{∘}$ and ${270}^{∘}$ are excluded values, and arranging the remaining solutions in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543538 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a135a2c5-e84d-52d5-89de-19fd76614f68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543538 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/25/2593d513ce130090a84cc26e71bf321960712a6ccb2d8ff82e55e4728cc0657c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Determine the exact values of the $x$-coordinates of points $A$ and $D,$ in radians.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa82973f-2912-57c5-acdd-b4b8b9abbe44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543542 #edb/ref 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives the equation $2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining the solutions and ordering them from smallest to largest, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n$\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n$\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543559 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf8eafa9-ba3d-54d7-ae84-18acaa32c81c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543559 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded value is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $  are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\pi =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df0bb882-2469-5cab-82f5-df4eee26a54c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543567 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded values are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTaking into account that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are excluded values, and arranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df31d07c-21a6-58c5-a19d-926033829f92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543568 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}$ has no solution.\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-274637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb877a39-4dd4-5455-8e16-f850f269ea03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543582 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={60}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={240}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543590 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543590 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543590 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543590 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543590 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f95ce679-5df8-536b-9a6e-bc83e05e288c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543590 #edb/ref 1074 "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\cdot 180=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc1ed235-5329-5107-b309-feec267a8cde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543592 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={45}^{∘},\\,{225}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={225}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
